// A bounded ring buffer: fixed storage, indices that cannot leave it, and the whole of what "this
// ring is consistent" means written into the types and the contracts rather than into the checks.
//
// The axis this owns is **the constrained-subtype surface** — `within` ranges, the `::` attributes
// they expose, `require`/`ensure`/`old`, and struct invariants. `guide/kernel` gives its three
// tables bounded identities and then never asks one a question; until this program nothing in the
// set had read an attribute or written a contract at all. A ring buffer is the smallest subject
// that needs all of them at once, because every bug a ring buffer has is an index that went
// somewhere it should not have, and those are the bugs a range type exists to make unwritable.
//
// It is written to be **compared**, like `guide/kernel` next to `guide/scheduler`: `Ring` and
// `Tracked` are the same buffer under the same scenarios, one keeping the fact of where the ring
// ends once and the other keeping it twice. Every check below runs both and asserts they agree, so
// the difference between the two implementations is the measurement.
//
// What that difference turned out to be, in the order it bit:
//
//   * **A derived subtype's attributes handed back its base**, which made the whole `::` surface
//     unusable by the only kind of subtype that needs it. `Slot::Last` was a `byte`, `Slot::Succ`
//     took one, and the loop variable of `Slot::Range` was one — so every use of an attribute had
//     to be cast back into `Slot`, undoing the `new` that made it a type at all. The sibling
//     surface next door had it right all along: a simple enum's `First`, `Last`, `Succ` and `Pred`
//     are the enum, and only the ones that cross into another domain (`Pos`, `Image`) are not. It
//     was invisible until now because a transparent subtype *is* its base, and every test the
//     attributes had used a transparent one. Fixed in the compiler — the attributes are the subtype,
//     and `Valid` alone takes the base, because asking whether a value is a `Slot` is only a
//     question about something that is not one yet.
//   * **The successor of the last slot is a trap, not a wrap.** `Slot::Succ` steps within the range
//     and refuses at `Last`, which is exactly right for a counter and exactly wrong for a ring —
//     the one operation a ring buffer is *made* of is the step that goes round. So the modular
//     successor is `wrap`, written by hand out of `%`, and the attribute set is not what supplies
//     the ring's own arithmetic. That is not a defect. It is what the attributes mean: `Succ` is
//     the step that stays inside a range, and a ring's step does not.
//   * **An invariant relating two fields is a claim about the representation, not about the code.**
//     `Tracked` keeps `head`, `tail` and `count`, so `tail == (head + count) % capacity` — and a
//     `push` that writes `tail` and then `count` traps at the first of the two, on a state that was
//     on its way to a perfectly good one. The reflex is to call that a wart and reach for a way to
//     suspend the check. It is not a wart: the invariant is the compiler pointing out that the
//     struct carries one fact twice, and `Ring` — `head` and `count`, with `tail` computed — has no
//     invariant to break because there is nothing left to disagree. The mid-update trap found a
//     redundant field. That is what it is for.
//
//     There are **two** honest two-field designs and the invariant rules out neither: this one, and
//     the embedded classic that keeps `head` and `tail` and derives the count, paying one unused
//     slot to tell a full ring from an empty one. That second one is what an interrupt-driven ring
//     wants, because a producer and a consumer then write disjoint fields and neither has to touch
//     a shared count. What the clause refuses is only the third design that keeps all of them.
//   * **Where the fields genuinely cannot be ordered, the way out costs the whole struct.** Keeping
//     `Tracked` anyway, the only form that moves two fields without being seen between them is
//     whole-struct assignment — `*self = Tracked(self.buf, ...)`, which restates the buffer to move
//     two bytes. For this ring that is thirty-two bytes copied per push against three written; for
//     a ring sized like a real one it is the entire storage, per element. **An invariant across two
//     fields makes the container's own update cost the size of the container**, and the check that
//     `Ring` and `Tracked` agree is bought at exactly that price.
//   * **Ordering the writes is enough more often than it looks.** `Watermark` relates two fields
//     too — `count <= high` — and needs none of the above, because raising the ceiling before the
//     floor keeps every intermediate state legal. The whole-struct form is the last resort, not the
//     first; the question to ask of a failing invariant is whether an order exists, and only then
//     whether the representation is redundant.
//   * **A program's own run cannot check its contracts; a test can.** A violated `require` traps,
//     so no check in `main.sysl` can be the one that breaks one — the program would die rather than
//     report, and the run would look truncated instead of failed. Every refusal the *run* asserts
//     therefore comes from a total operation that answers instead of trapping (`try_push`,
//     `try_pop`), and the contracts are asserted there only by the states they permit.
//
//     What the trap itself needs is somewhere that outlives the process it ends, and that is a
//     `#test(should_trap)` function: it runs in a process of its own and passes by not coming back.
//     They are in `tests.sysl`, beside the code they are about, and they are the reason the refusals
//     no longer have to be restated in a language the ring is not written in.
//
// Deliberately not modelled: growth, more than one producer, and any element type but `int`. The
// first two are other programs' axes and the third is `guide/hashmap`'s.

// The ring's one size. Both the storage and what may index it are named from it, so the two cannot
// drift apart — a bound is a constant expression, so `within 0..<capacity` is as ordinary as the
// array beside it.
const capacity: usize = 8

// A slot of the buffer, and how much of the buffer is occupied. They are `new`, so they are two
// types and not two names for `u8`: handing a count to something that wanted a slot does not
// compile, and that is the confusion a ring buffer actually makes. `Count` reaches `capacity`
// because a full ring has one; `Slot` does not, because there is no such slot.
type Slot = new u8 within 0..<capacity
type Count = new u8 within 0..capacity

// The step that goes round. `Slot::Succ` is the step that stays inside the range and traps at
// `Last`, which is the whole difference between a counter and a ring.
wrap(s: Slot) -> Slot = Slot(u8((usize(s) + 1) % capacity))

// Where the `n`th slot after `s` is.
advance(s: Slot, n: Count) -> Slot = Slot(u8((usize(s) + usize(n)) % capacity))

// The ring the invariant argued for: where the elements begin, and how many there are. Where they
// end is not a field, so there is no second copy of it to contradict the first, and no invariant
// relating fields — which is why every method below writes one field at a time and none of them
// has to move two things at once.
struct Ring
    buf: [capacity]int
    head: Slot
    count: Count

    // The end is computed. This is the field `Tracked` keeps, and the whole of what keeping it
    // costs is visible in the two implementations side by side.
    tail(self) -> Slot = advance(self.head, self.count)

    len(self) -> int = int(self.count)

    is_empty(self) -> bool = usize(self.count) == 0

    is_full(self) -> bool = usize(self.count) == capacity

    // The `i`th oldest element. The contract is on the caller because there is no answer to give
    // for an element that is not there — the total form is `try_pop`, and this is the checked one.
    at(self, i: int) -> int
        require i >= 0 && i < self.len()
        self.buf[usize(advance(self.head, Count(u8(i))))]
    end at

    // The postcondition is what `old` is for: the claim a push makes is not about the value it
    // stored but about the length before and after, which is a sentence that needs both.
    push(*self, v: int)
        require !self.is_full()
        ensure self.len() == old(self.len()) + 1
        self.buf[usize(self.tail())] = v
        self.count = Count(u8(usize(self.count) + 1))
    end push

    pop(*self) -> int
        require !self.is_empty()
        ensure self.len() == old(self.len()) - 1
        var v = self.buf[usize(self.head)]
        self.head = wrap(self.head)
        self.count = Count(u8(usize(self.count) - 1))
        v
    end pop

    // The total forms. A full ring is an ordinary answer rather than a broken caller, and these are
    // what the checks below use to assert a refusal — a violated `require` would end the program
    // instead of reporting.
    try_push(*self, v: int) -> bool
        if self.is_full() then return false
        self.push(v)
        true
    end try_push

    try_pop(*self) -> Option[int]
        if self.is_empty() then return None
        Some(self.pop())
    end try_pop

    peek(self) -> Option[int]
        if self.is_empty() then return None
        Some(self.buf[usize(self.head)])
    end peek

    clear(*self)
        ensure self.is_empty()
        self.count = Count(0u8)
    end clear

    // The elements oldest first, one digit each, which is what lets a check say what the ring
    // *contains* rather than only how much of it is occupied.
    contents(self) -> string
        var s = ""

        for i in 0..<self.len() do s += str(self.at(i))

        s
    end contents
end Ring

// The same ring keeping the end as a field as well. It behaves identically — every check runs both
// and compares — and the difference is entirely in how it is written.
struct Tracked
    buf: [capacity]int
    head: Slot
    tail: Slot
    count: Count
    invariant usize(tail) == (usize(head) + usize(count)) % capacity

    len(self) -> int = int(self.count)

    is_empty(self) -> bool = usize(self.count) == 0

    is_full(self) -> bool = usize(self.count) == capacity

    at(self, i: int) -> int
        require i >= 0 && i < self.len()
        self.buf[usize(advance(self.head, Count(u8(i))))]
    end at

    // Two fields have to move together and no order of the two is legal on its own, so the write
    // is of the whole struct — which restates the buffer to move two bytes. Writing
    // `self.tail = wrap(self.tail)` and then `self.count = ...` traps at the first of them.
    push(*self, v: int)
        require !self.is_full()
        ensure self.len() == old(self.len()) + 1
        self.buf[usize(self.tail)] = v
        *self = Tracked(self.buf, self.head, wrap(self.tail), Count(u8(usize(self.count) + 1)))
    end push

    pop(*self) -> int
        require !self.is_empty()
        ensure self.len() == old(self.len()) - 1
        var v = self.buf[usize(self.head)]
        *self = Tracked(self.buf, wrap(self.head), self.tail, Count(u8(usize(self.count) - 1)))
        v
    end pop

    try_push(*self, v: int) -> bool
        if self.is_full() then return false
        self.push(v)
        true
    end try_push

    try_pop(*self) -> Option[int]
        if self.is_empty() then return None
        Some(self.pop())
    end try_pop

    // Emptying moves the end as well, because the end is where the elements are — which is the
    // clearest single line of what the extra field costs: `Ring` writes `count` and is done.
    clear(*self)
        ensure self.is_empty()
        *self = Tracked(self.buf, self.head, self.head, Count(0u8))
    end clear

    contents(self) -> string
        var s = ""

        for i in 0..<self.len() do s += str(self.at(i))

        s
    end contents
end Tracked

// Two fields that must agree, where ordering the writes is the whole answer: raising the ceiling
// before the floor means no intermediate state is ever illegal. This is the case the mid-update
// trap does *not* apply to, and it is the common one.
struct Watermark
    count: Count
    high: Count
    invariant usize(count) <= usize(high)

    take(*self)
        require usize(self.count) < capacity
        ensure int(self.count) == int(old(self.count)) + 1
        var n = Count(u8(usize(self.count) + 1))

        if usize(n) > usize(self.high) then self.high = n

        self.count = n
    end take

    give(*self)
        require usize(self.count) > 0
        ensure int(self.count) == int(old(self.count)) - 1
        self.count = Count(u8(usize(self.count) - 1))
    end give
end Watermark

fresh_ring() -> Ring = Ring([0; capacity], Slot(0), Count(0))

fresh_tracked() -> Tracked = Tracked([0; capacity], Slot(0), Slot(0), Count(0))

// One thing a run does at a tick.
enum Step
    Put(v: int)
    Take
    Idle
end Step

// A producer and a consumer sharing one ring, stepping through a fixed script. Each tick writes a
// letter: the digit that moved, `x` for a push a full ring refused, `-` for a pop from an empty
// one, and `.` for a tick nobody used. The result is a schedule that can be worked out on paper,
// which is what makes it an assertion rather than a recording.
run_ring(script: []Step) -> string
    var r = fresh_ring()
    var out = ""

    for i in 0..<script.len
        out += script[i] match
            Put(v) -> if r.try_push(v) then str(v) else "x"
            Take -> r.try_pop() match
                Some(v) -> str(v)
                None -> "-"
            Idle -> "."

    out
end run_ring

// The same run over the ring that keeps its end twice. Every scenario below is asserted against
// both, and the two strings are required to be equal.
run_tracked(script: []Step) -> string
    var t = fresh_tracked()
    var out = ""

    for i in 0..<script.len
        out += script[i] match
            Put(v) -> if t.try_push(v) then str(v) else "x"
            Take -> t.try_pop() match
                Some(v) -> str(v)
                None -> "-"
            Idle -> "."

    out
end run_tracked
